1. Find the accepted family records
Replace the parent email:

select
  a.id as application_id,
  a.status,
  p.id as parent_record_id,
  p.email as parent_email,
  s.id as student_record_id,
  s.first_name as student_first_name,
  s.last_name as student_last_name,
  s.active as student_active
from public.applications a
join public.parents p on p.id = a.parent_id
join public.students s on s.id = a.student_id
where lower(p.email) = lower('REAL_PARENT_EMAIL');

Confirm:
status is accepted
student_active is true
The student name is correct
If not, accept and onboard the application from the administrator portal first.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Create Authentication users
Under Supabase → Authentication → Users, create or invite:
The parent using their real email
The student using a separate email, only if the student needs an independent login
The tutor using their real email
Never give the parent and student the same Authentication account.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Link the real parent
Change REAL_PARENT_EMAIL to the exact email used in both the application and Authentication:

begin;

update public.parents
set auth_user_id = (
  select id
  from auth.users
  where lower(email) = lower('REAL_PARENT_EMAIL')
)
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_PARENT_EMAIL');

insert into public.user_roles (user_id, role)
select id, 'parent'
from auth.users
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_PARENT_EMAIL')
on conflict (user_id) do update
set role = excluded.role;

commit;


Verify:


select
  p.id,
  p.first_name,
  p.last_name,
  p.email,
  p.auth_user_id,
  ur.role
from public.parents p
left join public.user_roles ur on ur.user_id = p.auth_user_id
where lower(p.email) = lower('REAL_PARENT_EMAIL');

Expected role: parent.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Link the real student
Copy student_record_id from step 1. Change both placeholders:

begin;

update public.students
set auth_user_id = (
  select id
  from auth.users
  where lower(email) = lower('REAL_STUDENT_LOGIN_EMAIL')
)
where id = 'STUDENT_RECORD_UUID';

insert into public.user_roles (user_id, role)
select id, 'student'
from auth.users
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_STUDENT_LOGIN_EMAIL')
on conflict (user_id) do update
set role = excluded.role;

commit;

Verify:

select
  s.id,
  s.first_name,
  s.last_name,
  s.active,
  au.email as login_email,
  ur.role
from public.students s
left join auth.users au on au.id = s.auth_user_id
left join public.user_roles ur on ur.user_id = s.auth_user_id
where s.id = 'STUDENT_RECORD_UUID';

Expected results:
active is true
login_email is the student’s email
role is student

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Link the real tutor
The tutor operational record should already exist and be active. Check it first:

select
  id as tutor_record_id,
  first_name,
  last_name,
  email,
  active,
  auth_user_id
from public.tutors
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_TUTOR_EMAIL');


Then link it:


begin;

update public.tutors
set
  auth_user_id = (
    select id
    from auth.users
    where lower(email) = lower('REAL_TUTOR_EMAIL')
  ),
  active = true
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_TUTOR_EMAIL');

insert into public.user_roles (user_id, role)
select id, 'tutor'
from auth.users
where lower(email) = lower('REAL_TUTOR_EMAIL')
on conflict (user_id) do update
set role = excluded.role;

commit;


Verify:


select
  t.id,
  t.first_name,
  t.last_name,
  t.email,
  t.active,
  t.auth_user_id,
  ur.role
from public.tutors t
left join public.user_roles ur on ur.user_id = t.auth_user_id
where lower(t.email) = lower('REAL_TUTOR_EMAIL');



Expected results:
active is true
auth_user_id is populated
role is tutor

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Verify the tutor/student assignment

select
  s.first_name || ' ' || s.last_name as student,
  t.first_name || ' ' || t.last_name as tutor,
  sta.active
from public.student_tutor_assignments sta
join public.students s on s.id = sta.student_id
join public.tutors t on t.id = sta.tutor_id
where s.id = 'STUDENT_RECORD_UUID';

There should be one row with active = true. If there is no row, use the administrator portal to assign the tutor to the accepted student.
